\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Polynomial observables and positive expectations}
\date{2026-10-16}

\p{For the \vocabk{Gaussian probability law}{fdip-0005} #{\mu_Q},
an \newvocab{integrable observable} is a [Borel measurable function](fdip-002F)
#{F:X\to\mathbb R} with #{\int_X|F|\,d\mu_Q<\infty}.
Its \newvocab{Gaussian expectation} is
##{\begin{aligned}
\langle F\rangle_Q&=\int_X F\,d\mu_Q\\
&=\frac{1}{Z_Q}\int_X F(\phi)w_Q(\phi)\,d\phi.
\end{aligned}}
This is the continuous version of an
\vocabk{expectation}{ftip-000A}; absolute integrability replaces the
automatic convergence of a finite sum. The real-spin integration
convention is \citet{Definition 8.1 and Equation (8.3), p. 381}{friedli2017statistical}.}

\p{Let #{\mathcal P(X)} be the real algebra of
[polynomial functions](fdip-002A) generated by the
coordinate functions #{\phi\mapsto\phi_v}. Every member is integrable.
On this algebra, #{P\mapsto\langle P\rangle_Q} is a
\vocabk{real algebraic state}{fdip-0024}: it is real linear, sends the
constant one to one, and satisfies
##{\langle P^2\rangle_Q\geq 0\qquad(P\in\mathcal P(X)).}
More generally, a pointwise nonnegative polynomial has nonnegative
expectation. The following bound proves the polynomial domain using the
orthogonal coordinates of
\citet{Lemma B.54, p. 504}{friedli2017statistical}.}

\proof{
\p{For #{k\geq 0} an integer and #{a>0}, choose an integer #{M\geq 0}
with #{2M\geq k}, and put #{c=a/4}. For #{r\geq 1}, the exponential
series gives
##{\begin{aligned}
\exp(c r^2)&\geq\frac{(c r^2)^M}{M!},\\
r^k\exp(-c r^2)&\leq M!c^{-M}.
\end{aligned}}
For #{0\leq r\leq 1}, the left side of the second inequality is at most
one. Thus, with #{C=\max(1,M!c^{-M})},
##{\begin{aligned}
&|x|^k\exp(-a x^2/2)\\
&\quad\leq C\exp(-a x^2/4).
\end{aligned}}
The right side is integrable by the one-dimensional estimate in
\ref{fdip-0005}. This includes #{k=M=0}.}

\p{Use #{\phi=Uy} and the positive eigenvalues #{\lambda_i} from
\ref{fdip-0005}. A polynomial remains a finite sum of monomials after
this substitution, with nonnegative integer exponents #{\alpha_i}:
##{P(Uy)=\sum_\alpha c_\alpha\prod_{i=1}^n y_i^{\alpha_i}.}
Put #{g_i(y_i)=\exp(-\lambda_i y_i^2/2)}.
The triangle inequality gives
##{\begin{aligned}
&|P(Uy)|\prod_i g_i(y_i)\\
&\quad\leq\sum_\alpha|c_\alpha|
      \prod_i\left(|y_i|^{\alpha_i}g_i(y_i)\right).
\end{aligned}}
Each product has finite integral by Tonelli and the one-dimensional bound.
The sum is finite, and the [orthogonal substitution preserves measure](fdip-002L).
Hence #{\int_X|P|w_Q\,d\phi<\infty}. This applies to #{P^2} as well.
Linearity and order positivity now follow from the integral, while
#{\langle 1\rangle_Q=Z_Q/Z_Q=1}.}
}

\p{Constants have #{\langle c\rangle_Q=c}. Since
#{S_Q(-\phi)=S_Q(\phi)}, the substitution #{\phi\mapsto-\phi}
preserves the measure and weight. For any integrable odd observable,
#{F(-\phi)=-F(\phi)}, it gives
#{\langle F\rangle_Q=-\langle F\rangle_Q}, hence
#{\langle F\rangle_Q=0}. In particular,
#{\langle\phi_v\rangle_Q=0} for every vertex.}

\p{The \vocabk{covariance}{ftip-008H} of two coordinate fields is the
centered two-point expectation
##{\begin{aligned}
&\operatorname{Cov}_Q(u,v)\\
&\quad=\left\langle
      (\phi_u-\langle\phi_u\rangle_Q)
      (\phi_v-\langle\phi_v\rangle_Q)
     \right\rangle_Q\\
&\quad=\langle\phi_u\phi_v\rangle_Q.
\end{aligned}}
It is well-defined by polynomial integrability. The partition function
#{Z_Q}, this two-point expectation, and an expectation of another
observable are different quantities. Computing the covariance requires a
further argument.}

\p{The larger space of all integrable observables is a real vector
space, but need not be closed under multiplication; the state above is
asserted on the polynomial algebra. Nor is every function integrable:
#{F=\exp(S_Q)} has #{Fw_Q=1}, whose integral over #{X} is infinite.}

\p{TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.integrable_eval_mul_gaussianEnvelope}
proves integrability of a one-variable polynomial times a positive-variance
Gaussian envelope. The finite products and orthogonal substitution above
extend that component statement to the polynomial domain used here.}
